WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.580
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:09.470
Due to popular request, here's a more intuitive
explanation of our latest work.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:14.460
Believe it or not, when I have started working
on this, Two Minute Papers didn't even exist.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.210
In several research areas, there are cases
where we can't talk about our work until it

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:20.210
is published.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:24.780
I knew that the paper would not see the light
of the day for quite a while, if ever, so

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.710
I started Two Minute Papers to be able to
keep my sanity and deliver a hopefully nice

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.090
piece of work on a regular basis.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:37.590
In the end, this took more than 3000 work
hours to complete, but it is finally here,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.160
and I am so happy to finally be able to present
it to you.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.910
This work is in the intersection of computer
graphics and AI, which you know is among my

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:46.910
favorites.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:48.120
So what do we see here?

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.830
This beautiful scene contains more than a
100 different materials, each of which has

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.930
been learned and synthesized by an AI.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.830
None of these daisies and dandelions are alike,
each of them have a different material model.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:06.820
The goal is to teach an AI the concept of
material models such as metals, minerals and

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.610
translucent materials.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.780
Traditionally, when we are looking to create
a new material model with a light simulation

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.530
program, we have to fiddle with quite a few
parameters, and whenever we change something,

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.790
we have to wait from 40 to 60 seconds until
a noise-free result appears.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.060
In our solution, we don't need to play with
these parameters.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:30.820
Instead, our goal is to grab a gallery of
random materials, assign a score to each of

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.780
them saying that I liked this one, I didn't
like that one, and get an AI to learn our

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.920
preferences and recommend new materials for
us.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:44.250
This is quite useful when we’re looking
to synthesize not only one, but many materials.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.850
So this is learning algorithm number one,
and it works really well for a variety of

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.850
materials.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.810
However, these recommendations still have
to be rendered with a light simulation program,

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.880
which takes several hours for a gallery like
the one you see here.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.659
Here comes learning algorithm number two to
the rescue, a neural network that replaces

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:07.510
this light simulation program and creates
photorealistic visualizations.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:13.810
It is so fast, it not only does this in real
time, but it is more than 10 times faster

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.090
than real time.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.130
We call this a neural renderer.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.611
So we have a lot of material recommendations,
and they are all photorealistic that we can

00:02:21.611 --> 00:02:23.580
visualize in real time.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.480
However, it is always a possibility that we
have a recommendation that is almost exactly

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.200
what we had in mind, but need a few adjustments.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.220
That’s an issue, because to do that, we
would have to go back to the parameter fiddling,

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.790
which we really wanted to avoid in the first
place.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:43.090
No worries, because the third learning algorithm
is coming to the rescue.

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:48.310
What this can do is take our favorite material
models from the gallery, and map them onto

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:52.349
a nice 2D plane where we can explore similar
materials.

00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:57.069
If we combine this with the neural renderer,
we can explore these photorealistic visualizations

00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:01.120
and everything is appears not in a few hours,
but in real time.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.620
However, without a little further guidance,
we get a bit lost because we still don’t

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:10.250
know which regions in this 2D space are going
to give us materials that are similar to the

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.099
one we wish to fine-tune.

00:03:12.099 --> 00:03:17.380
We can further improve this by exploring different
combinations of the three learning algorithms.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.880
In the end, we can assign these colors to
the background that describe either whether

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:30.060
the AI expects us to like the output, or how
similar the output will be.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.860
A nice use-case of this is where we have this
glassy still life scene, but the color of

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:38.230
the grapes is a bit too vivid for us.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:51.000
Now, we can go to this 2D latent space, and
adjust it to our liking in real time.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.230
Much better.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:55.060
No material modeling expertise is required.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.540
So I hope you have found this explanation
intuitive.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:03.150
We tried really hard to create something that
is both scientifically novel and also useful

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.050
for the computer game and motion picture industry.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:10.269
We had to throw away hundreds of other ideas
until this final system materialized.

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:14.510
Make sure to have a look at the paper in the
description where every single element and

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:18.359
learning algorithm is tested and evaluated
one by one.

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:22.509
If you are a journalist and you would like
to write about this work, I would be most

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:26.729
grateful, and I am also more than happy to
answer questions in an interview format as

00:04:26.729 --> 00:04:27.729
well.

00:04:27.729 --> 00:04:28.849
Please reach out if you’re interested.

00:04:28.849 --> 00:04:32.911
We also try to give back to the community,
so for the fellow tinkerers out there, the

00:04:32.911 --> 00:04:38.560
entirety of the paper is under the permissive
Creative Commons license, and the full source

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.620
code and pre-trained neural networks are also
available under the even more permissive MIT

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.620
license.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.830
Everyone is welcome to reuse it or build something
cool on top of it.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:51.490
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

